Don’t outsource the learning
The best evidence so far comes from a field experiment with about a thousand high-school maths students (Bastani et al., 2025). During practice sessions, students had either no AI, a standard ChatGPT-style interface (GPT Base), or a GPT-4 tutor with safeguards designed by teachers that gave hints instead of answers (GPT Tutor).
During practice, both AI groups did much better than the control group: GPT Base by 48% and GPT Tutor by 127%. Then the AI was taken away for the exam. GPT Base students scored 17% lower than students who never had AI. GPT Tutor students showed no significant drop.
It is a different setting from an MPhil, but the mechanism, using the tool as a crutch, carries over. On the course, as in that exam, the AI will be removed at some point. Ways to use it that build skill:

try first, then ask it to explain;
ask for hints, not answers;
have it quiz you or set practice problems;
get feedback on an interpretation you wrote yourself.

Ways that quietly replace skill:

letting it choose the model you are meant to learn to choose;
reading its interpretation before writing your own;
using it instead of your trial statistician;
anything your assessment does not permit.

If you couldn’t do it without the LLM, you are not ready to delegate it.

Debugging Shiny Applications
Debugging Shiny can be challenging. Key approaches include:

Use browser() in reactive contexts:


library(shiny)

ui &lt;- fluidPage(
    numericInput("num", "Number:", 10),
    textOutput("result")
)

server &lt;- function(input, output) {
    output$result &lt;- renderText({
        browser()
        paste("Double:", input$num * 2)
    })
}

shinyApp(ui, server)


Monitor logs and console outputs.

Profiling Computational Performance
Optimising R code begins with understanding where time and resources are spent. Profiling helps identify bottlenecks - functions or operations that consume excessive computation time. By analysing execution time, memory usage, and function calls, we can make targeted improvements to enhance efficiency.

Why Profiling Matters?

Detects slow functions that need optimisation.

Highlights redundant computations or excessive loops.

Helps determine whether vectorisation or parallelisation could improve performance.

Identifies memory-intensive operations that could cause inefficiencies.



Example: Using profvis() to Analyse Execution Time
library(profvis)

function_1 &lt;- function(size) {
  large_list &lt;- lapply(1:size, function(x) rnorm(5000))
  return(large_list)
}

function_2 &lt;- function(iterations) {
  result &lt;- 0
  for (i in 1:iterations) {
    Sys.sleep(0.1) 
    result &lt;- result + i
  }
  return(result)
}

profvis({
  function_1(10000)
  function_2(30)
})


Explanation:

profvis() visualises where time is spent in an R script.
Helps identify slow functions and optimise code for better efficiency.

Talk · BSU Together, MRC Biostatistics Unit, Cambridge


Slides Source code

A short talk about what a statistical programmer does in a methods group, using my work on the MAMS package as the example. It covers:

what goes into a pkgdown site and how one is built from a package’s help files and vignettes;
the MAMS documentation site we built this way;
interactive output in slides, shown with a Plotly animation of the central limit theorem;
webR, which runs R inside the browser, so readers can edit and run MAMS examples without installing anything.

On webR, the gains are near-native speed in the browser and nothing to install, neither R on the reader’s computer nor a server. The limits at the time were that not every CRAN package was available, code runs on a single core, and access to local files is restricted.
The slides are a Quarto revealjs presentation styled after the BSU template, with a live webR console. They are embedded below; press F for full screen, or open them in a new tab. R takes a few seconds to load before the code cells run.

Talk · Group meeting, MRC Biostatistics Unit


Slides (PDF)

The session started from a familiar problem: one of the unit’s HPC accounts had gone over its CPU-hour limit. Amazon EC2 is one way to keep simulations running in the meantime. I went through the steps I followed:

choosing a region and an instance type from the price list (for example 64 vCPUs in the London region), and using the EC2 instance type finder, which recommended compute-optimised Graviton instances (C7g, C7gd, C8g) for numerical simulations;
the free tier and the starting credits for new accounts;
launching an instance from a template, connecting over SSH and checking the load with htop;
installing R on Debian and saving the configured machine as an Amazon Machine Image (AMI), so the next machine starts with R 4.5.2 already set up.
